<p>Start with what makes this different from the dog version of this question: dogs pant. It's normal, constant, and usually not a symptom of anything. Cats don't really do that. A cat sitting in a sunny window or batting a toy for ten minutes might pant briefly afterward, and that's not automatically alarming. But a cat who's just resting, sleeping, or purring on the couch and breathing fast or hard is showing you something dogs don't show nearly as often at rest: a sign that's genuinely more likely to mean something is wrong.</p>

<p>That distinction matters because cats are, famously, bad at looking sick. They hide pain and illness as a survival instinct left over from being both predator and prey, and by the time a cat "looks" obviously unwell, the underlying problem has often been building for a while. Breathing rate is one of the few windows into that process you can check yourself, with no equipment, in under a minute.</p>

<h2>Cat Breathing Fast? Count Before You Panic</h2>

<p>Here's the actual exercise, and it's worth doing now, before anything seems wrong, so you know your own cat's normal: wait until your cat is fully at rest, asleep or just quietly lying down, not purring, not just finished playing or eating, and not stressed by something like a vet visit or a car ride. Watch one side of her chest or belly rise and fall. Count how many full breaths happen in 60 seconds, or count for 30 seconds and double it.</p>

<p>Most sources describe a healthy adult cat's resting respiratory rate as falling somewhere in the 15-to-30-breaths-per-minute range, with 20 to 30 being the range you'll see cited most often for a relaxed, sleeping cat. Where this becomes more than trivia is the number Cornell's Feline Health Center attaches to actual concern: in cats with heart disease, an accelerated resting respiratory rate above 35 breaths per minute is flagged as a warning sign, and rapid or labored breathing in that context is described as a medical emergency, not a wait-and-see situation.</p>

<p>So the useful takeaway isn't a single hard line, it's this: know what your own cat runs at rest when she's healthy, and treat a resting rate that's climbed well past 30, especially one pushing past 35, as a reason to call your vet rather than keep monitoring on your own.</p>

<h2>"But She's Acting Totally Normal"</h2>

<p>This is the pattern that trips people up, and it's a common enough search that it's clearly happening to a lot of cat owners: fast breathing while the cat is otherwise eating, playing, purring, seemingly fine. It's tempting to read "acting normal" as "nothing's wrong," and sometimes that's right, a cat can run a slightly elevated rate after being startled or briefly after rough play.</p>

<p>But "acting normal" in a cat carries less reassurance than it does in a dog, precisely because masking distress is what cats are built to do. A cat with early heart disease or in the early stages of an asthma flare can still eat, still purr, still seem like herself, right up until the breathing problem suddenly isn't subtle anymore. Purring itself doesn't rule out fast breathing being meaningful; a cat can purr while breathing abnormally, so "she was purring" isn't evidence the rate you counted doesn't matter.</p>

<p>The practical rule: if you counted a resting breathing rate that's clearly elevated, especially one sustained over more than a few minutes or one that shows up repeatedly, call your vet and describe the number, even if your cat seems completely fine in every other way. A phone call to describe a counted rate costs you nothing. Waiting to see if it gets worse costs time you may not have if it's one of the two causes below.</p>

<h2>The Two Causes Worth Actually Knowing About</h2>

<p>A cat's breathing can speed up for plenty of reasons, fever, pain, anemia, stress, heat. But two specific causes come up again and again in cats, are both capable of showing fast breathing as an early or only sign, and are both serious enough that it's worth understanding how they differ.</p>

<h3>Feline Asthma</h3>

<p>Cornell describes feline asthma as a disease of the lower airways triggered by inhaled allergens, things like cigarette smoke, dust, pollen, mold, or strong cleaning fumes, that cause the airways to inflame, swell, narrow, and fill with mucus. It's a chronic condition with flare-ups rather than a single illness that resolves. Signs include wheezing, coughing or hacking that can look like your cat is trying to bring up a hairball, rapid breathing, open-mouthed breathing, and during a more severe attack, crouching low with the neck stretched forward, working visibly hard to move air. Cornell notes it's manageable in most cats long-term, but it isn't curable, and flare-ups can escalate quickly without warning.</p>

<h3>Hypertrophic Cardiomyopathy (and Heart Disease Generally)</h3>

<p>This is the one that deserves the most attention, because it's both common and quiet. Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, a thickening of the heart's muscular walls, is the most common heart disease in cats, and Cornell cites it as affecting up to 15% of the feline population, with certain breeds, including Maine Coons, Ragdolls, Sphynxes, British Shorthairs, Chartreux, and Persians, at higher predisposition. Plenty of cats live close to normal lives with it and never need treatment. The problem is that cardiomyopathies in general are often silent early on; Cornell's own material notes that many affected cats show no signs at all in the earlier stages of the disease.</p>

<p>When symptoms do show up, breathing is frequently the first thing an owner notices, because fluid building up in the lungs from congestive heart failure makes a cat breathe harder and faster, often alongside lethargy and reduced appetite. A cat with underlying heart disease can also throw a blood clot that lodges in the arteries supplying the hind legs, causing sudden pain or paralysis; it's a separate complication, but part of the same disease, and an emergency in its own right.</p>

<p>The combination that makes this genuinely more urgent in cats than the breathing issue is in dogs: a cat can carry fairly advanced heart disease without a single visible symptom, and then breathing changes can be the first and only warning before things escalate. That's a real reason to take a counted resting rate seriously rather than waiting for other symptoms to confirm it.</p>

<h3>Asthma vs. Heart Disease: Where the Signs Overlap and Differ</h3>

<p>Both conditions can produce fast or labored breathing, and you can't reliably tell them apart from symptoms alone, only a vet exam, chest imaging, and sometimes an echocardiogram can do that. But a few features tend to lean one way or the other:</p>

<ul>
<li><strong>Coughing:</strong> Common with asthma, frequently mistaken for a hairball attempt. Uncommon with heart disease in cats, where coughing is a less typical sign than it is in dogs with heart failure.</li>
<li><strong>Wheezing:</strong> More associated with asthma's narrowed airways.</li>
<li><strong>Sudden hind-limb pain or weakness:</strong> Points toward a cardiac blood clot, not asthma.</li>
<li><strong>Lethargy and appetite loss alongside fast breathing:</strong> More typical of heart failure presenting with fluid buildup, though a cat mid-asthma-attack can also seem subdued simply from the effort of breathing.</li>
<li><strong>Breed:</strong> Maine Coons, Ragdolls, and the other breeds listed above carry higher cardiomyopathy risk, which can nudge a vet's suspicion, though any cat of any breed can develop either condition.</li>
</ul>

<p>None of these are rules, and plenty of cases don't sort cleanly. The point of knowing the overlap isn't to self-diagnose, it's to describe what you're seeing accurately when you call the vet, which helps them triage how fast you need to come in.</p>

<h2>Open-Mouth Breathing Is a Bigger Flag in Cats Than in Dogs</h2>

<p>A dog panting with its mouth open after a walk is unremarkable. A cat breathing with its mouth open, outside of brief heat-related panting after exertion, is not. Cornell lists open-mouthed breathing as one of the signs of a feline asthma attack, and it shows up with cardiac distress too. If you see a resting cat breathing through an open mouth, especially paired with a fast rate, crouched posture, or extended neck, treat it as more urgent than you would a panting dog, not less. This isn't a "monitor overnight" situation; it's a same-day or emergency call.</p>

<p>Gum and tongue color is worth a quick glance too. Pale, grey, or bluish gums alongside breathing trouble mean the cat isn't getting enough oxygen, and that combination needs emergency care immediately.</p>

<h2>What to Actually Do</h2>

<p>If you've counted a resting rate that's clearly elevated, or you've seen open-mouth breathing, labored effort, or blue-tinged gums, the move is to call your vet or an emergency clinic and describe exactly what you saw, including the number you counted if you have one. Don't try to calm a struggling cat by picking her up or restraining her for a closer look; cats in respiratory distress can decompensate from the stress of handling alone, so minimize handling and get her into a carrier calmly, then go. If your cat is also showing signs of not eating or unusual lethargy alongside the breathing changes, our <a href="/tools/cat-not-eating-guide/">cat not eating guide</a> can help you sort out how urgent the overall picture is. And if you're ever unsure in the moment whether something qualifies as an emergency, our <a href="/tools/pet-first-aid-guide/">pet first aid guide</a> is a reasonable place to check while you decide whether to go now or call ahead.</p>

<p>If you have a dog in the house too and want the comparison, our companion piece on <a href="/blog/dog-breathing-fast-heavy-panting/">why dogs breathe fast or heavy</a> covers that side of things, but keep the key difference in mind: a panting dog at rest is often completely unremarkable, while a panting or open-mouth-breathing cat at rest usually is not. Don't apply the dog read to the cat sitting next to it.</p>

<h2>The Bottom Line</h2>

<p>Cats don't pant the way dogs do, so fast or labored breathing in a resting, sleeping, or purring cat carries more weight than it would in a dog doing the same thing, and "acting normal" otherwise isn't a guarantee that nothing's wrong, because masking illness is exactly what cats are good at. Learn what your own cat's resting rate looks like when she's healthy, recheck it if something feels off, and treat a clearly elevated count, or any open-mouth breathing, as a reason to call rather than wait. Feline asthma and hypertrophic cardiomyopathy are the two causes worth knowing by name, because both are common, both can show breathing changes as an early or only sign, and both respond better to care the earlier they're caught.</p>

